1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:05,670
When I was one month old, I lost
about 90 to 95% of my hearing.

2
00:00:05,750 --> 00:00:08,290
I’ve worn hearing aids ever since.

3
00:00:08,370 --> 00:00:13,910
They made accessibility a lot easier,
but they don’t solve every problem.

4
00:00:14,790 --> 00:00:20,950
One problem is that you can’t hear some
frequencies of sound, like consonants.

5
00:00:21,030 --> 00:00:27,040
That makes it hard to tell these sounds
apart: s, f, th, sh, ch, k, p and h.

6
00:00:27,920 --> 00:00:31,600
So when you’re a kid and
you can’t hear them, it’s

7
00:00:31,680 --> 00:00:34,380
hard to learn how to pronounce words.

8
00:00:34,460 --> 00:00:37,990
For the longest time, I
said “chair” as “shair”,

9
00:00:38,070 --> 00:00:40,930
because I couldn’t hear the difference.

10
00:00:41,810 --> 00:00:43,970
It took a lot of speech therapy.

11
00:00:44,050 --> 00:00:48,380
Trying to say what you literally
can’t hear was frustrating and

12
00:00:48,460 --> 00:00:52,510
time-consuming for the child,
the parent and the therapist.

13
00:00:53,390 --> 00:00:59,490
It’s like doing maths with your dad at
the dinner table, but ten times worse.

14
00:00:59,570 --> 00:01:02,460
I wouldn’t wish it on my worst enemy.

15
00:01:02,540 --> 00:01:06,790
I’m traumatised to this day,
and I’m not the only one.

16
00:01:07,670 --> 00:01:12,740
Research shows that children with
hearing loss are slowest to learn sounds

17
00:01:12,820 --> 00:01:16,860
like S and SH, because hearing
aids can’t fully carry them.

18
00:01:16,940 --> 00:01:22,220
They’re asked to say sounds they can’t
hear, with no way to check themselves.

19
00:01:23,100 --> 00:01:26,630
So I came up with a way
to visualise your voice.

20
00:01:26,710 --> 00:01:31,820
Soundbook is a gamified storytelling
app for deaf and hard of hearing

21
00:01:31,900 --> 00:01:36,110
children, where they can see
what their voice looks like.

22
00:01:36,990 --> 00:01:40,700
The parent or therapist
picks the family of sounds

23
00:01:40,780 --> 00:01:43,280
they want their child to practise.

24
00:01:43,360 --> 00:01:46,540
Each family of sounds
is linked to a story.

25
00:01:47,420 --> 00:01:51,360
They pick a story and read
it aloud with their child.

26
00:01:51,440 --> 00:01:56,970
Throughout the story, the child is
asked to make letter sounds, like this.

27
00:01:57,850 --> 00:01:58,910
Sssssss…

28
00:01:58,990 --> 00:02:02,200
I need practice, y’all.

29
00:02:02,280 --> 00:02:06,470
I can’t do it at my grown age.

30
00:02:07,350 --> 00:02:11,610
The slider shows the child how
soft or sharp their voice is,

31
00:02:11,690 --> 00:02:14,800
which is very different
from quiet and loud.

32
00:02:14,880 --> 00:02:20,370
They can see the difference between two
sounds, and get better with practice.

33
00:02:21,250 --> 00:02:24,480
The child’s voice moves the plot forward.

34
00:02:24,560 --> 00:02:28,510
Here, Sid and his friend
need to pick where to go.

35
00:02:28,590 --> 00:02:31,900
The child can make either sound to choose.

36
00:02:31,980 --> 00:02:34,970
I choose Shell Beach, so I say “Shhh”…

37
00:02:35,050 --> 00:02:36,180
and there I go.

38
00:02:37,060 --> 00:02:40,660
Soundbook doesn’t replace
speech therapy or human support.

39
00:02:40,740 --> 00:02:44,530
It’s a tool within speech therapy
that makes learning faster,

40
00:02:44,610 --> 00:02:47,380
and more fun, for children
with hearing loss.

41
00:02:48,260 --> 00:02:51,850
AI served as an accelerator
for research and for exploring

42
00:02:51,930 --> 00:02:55,640
different directions, but human
judgement was always needed.

43
00:02:55,720 --> 00:03:00,890
It showed us many directions, and we judged
which ones actually worked in practice.

44
00:03:01,770 --> 00:03:05,410
Claude generated a lot of
options quickly: particle stars,

45
00:03:05,490 --> 00:03:08,680
glowing orbs, glass sliders,
dark and light themes.

46
00:03:08,760 --> 00:03:11,960
We asked whether each one
would actually work for a

47
00:03:12,040 --> 00:03:14,400
five-year-old with a cochlear implant.

48
00:03:15,280 --> 00:03:19,070
The particle star was
beautiful, but children couldn’t

49
00:03:19,150 --> 00:03:21,720
tell which way to change their sound.

50
00:03:21,800 --> 00:03:25,600
So we moved to a simple
slider between the two sounds.

51
00:03:26,480 --> 00:03:30,760
The glass knob looked nice
but was hard to see, so we

52
00:03:30,840 --> 00:03:33,720
made it a plain, high-contrast ring.

53
00:03:34,600 --> 00:03:37,670
Dark mode looked polished,
but a bright storybook

54
00:03:37,750 --> 00:03:39,920
felt friendlier for young children.

55
00:03:40,800 --> 00:03:44,210
We didn’t just take the
suggestions as they were.

56
00:03:44,290 --> 00:03:48,850
We added our own ideas, like
one-direction pages, a dotted target

57
00:03:48,930 --> 00:03:52,270
zone, and a silhouette
of who’s about to appear.

58
00:03:53,150 --> 00:03:55,770
And we redrew the characters ourselves.

59
00:03:55,850 --> 00:03:59,300
AI gave us breadth; our
judgement gave us the right

60
00:03:59,380 --> 00:04:01,940
answer, and pushed the quality higher.

61
00:04:02,820 --> 00:04:08,140
Thank you for watching, and thank you
for hosting the SVSD design competition.
